public class Main{
    public static void main(String[] args){
        System.out.println("Welcome to the ATM Application");
        System.out.println("Welcome to the ATM Application");
        System.out.println("Welcome to the ATM Application");
    }
}


Benefits of using methods
1. Code Reusability: Methods allow you to write a block of code once and reuse it multiple times throughout your program. This reduces redundancy and makes your code more efficient.
2. Modularity: Methods help in breaking down a complex program into smaller, manageable pieces. Each method can perform a specific task, making the code easier to understand and maintain.
3. Better reliability: By encapsulating specific functionality within methods, you can isolate and test individual components of your program. This improves the overall reliability of your code.
4. Easy Maintenance: When you need to make changes or updates to your code, having methods allows you to modify specific parts without affecting the entire program. This makes maintenance easier and less error-prone.
5. Reduces Duplicate Code: Methods help in reducing duplicate code by allowing you to reuse the same logic in different parts of your program. This leads to cleaner and more concise code.
6. Easy Testing: Methods can be tested independently, making it easier to identify and fix issues. You can write unit tests for individual methods to ensure they work as expected.


